================================================================================
  TIMETABLE DATABASE SCHEMA
  Database: MongoDB (Atlas)  ·  DB name: hacky_wacky
================================================================================


COLLECTION: users
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Stores the registered user base.

  Field        Type        Description
  ───────────  ──────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  _id          String      Unique user ID (UUID)
  name         String      User's display name
  email        String      Email address (unique)


COLLECTION: timetable_blocks
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Stores every block in a user's timetable — both manually entered events and
scheduler-generated study sessions.

  Field        Type        Description
  ───────────  ──────────  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  _id          ObjectId    MongoDB auto-generated ID
  block_id     String      Application-level UUID (unique index)
  user_id      String      FK → users._id
  title        String      Human-readable label (e.g. "CS101 Lecture")
  block_type   String      One of the BlockType enum values:
                             lecture, seminar, lab, society, sleep,
                             sport, work, personal, study_session, other
  start_time   DateTime    Start of the block (UTC)
  end_time     DateTime    End of the block (UTC)
  notes        String?     Optional free-text notes
  attended     Boolean?    null = unknown/future, true/false = recorded
  source       String      *** KEY FIELD — see below ***
                             "fixed"     → user manually entered
                             "generated" → created by the scheduler


INDEXES
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Index                                         Type      Purpose
  ────────────────────────────────────────────   ────────  ─────────────────────
  (block_id)                                    Unique    Upsert by block ID
  (user_id, source)                             Compound  Filter by source type
  (user_id, start_time, end_time)               Compound  Time-range queries


THE "source" FIELD — FIXED vs GENERATED
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Every timetable block is tagged with a source:

  "fixed"       Blocks the user created themselves — lectures, sleep,
                societies, personal events, etc.  These are NEVER touched
                by the scheduler.

  "generated"   Study sessions produced by the scheduling algorithm.
                These can be safely deleted and regenerated at any time:

                    db.timetable_blocks.delete_many({
                        "user_id": "<id>",
                        "source": "generated"
                    })

This distinction means:
  • Editing free time or re-running the scheduler only wipes "generated"
    blocks — the user's original inputs remain intact.
  • The scheduler reads back only "fixed" blocks to know what's occupied,
    then fills gaps with new "generated" study sessions.


API FUNCTIONS (database/timetable_db.py)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  get_user_schedule(user_id, *, source=None)
      → list[TimeBlock]
      Reads all blocks for a user, optionally filtered by source.
      Sorted by start_time ascending.

  add_blocks(user_id, blocks, source="fixed")
      → int
      Upserts blocks for a user. Returns count of documents written.
      source must be "fixed" or "generated".

  clear_generated_blocks(user_id)
      → int
      Deletes all "generated" blocks for a user. Returns deleted count.
      Call before re-running the scheduler.

  ensure_indexes()
      Creates the indexes listed above (idempotent).

================================================================================
